ment but that  maybe that he's entitled to do that that  he should be right um i think that's a  tragedy um the third one is that the  evidence of self-defense was  pretty to i would say very strong  actually when you look at the facts of  the case um  and then  castro tweeted this and i thought was  interesting is that if cal britton house  was black  we're probably having a different  conversation and there's a probably a  significantly higher chance he's in jail  um  i mean there's a higher chance that he  would have been shot on the scene if  he'd been like you know  like there is an issue where he was  walking past law enforcement with his  hands up and they were supporting him  yeah yeah so encouraging him so so that  those things are real um but  no  i don't think that it's appropriate to  want a verdict uh to to  be in keeping with like a particular  um  message i mean it is a person it's a  court of law if the person's  uh to be acquitted on the basis of the  law i mean you have to accept that in my  view  um so it's a very tough situation it  makes me sad it makes me sad for the  families of the  people who lost their lives uh it makes  me sad that our country  um is trying to make  a cause out of what in my view is  something that a very very  you know irresponsible immature 17 year  old did like lionizing that that kid is  a hero strikes me as irresponsible on  itself  no one of the the struggles that i have  very often  is that  you see something happen particularly in  this age of social media and then you  magnify it and you can look at this  incident say oh  there's like a race war or civil war and  and that's a narrative that the media  does love and love to fuel and i i try  and remove myself at least a couple  steps and say wait like is this like an  army of  people all organized or is this just one  in this case you know 17 year old with  terrible ideas  and  you know and then the the reverse  happens in waukesha wisconsin and you  can say look i